# CURSOR RULES — Enochian: The Celestial Archive
# Read GPT_ARCHITECT_BRIEF.md before starting any session.

## PROJECT IDENTITY

You are building an immersive educational app about the Enochian magick system.
It is NOT a wiki. NOT a SaaS dashboard. NOT a generic content platform.
It should feel like entering a Renaissance occult archive — ancient, alive, precise.

The governing document is: /docs/GPT_ARCHITECT_BRIEF.md
The visual spec is: /docs/VISUAL_DIRECTION.md
The locked scope is: /docs/MVP_SCOPE.md
The voice guide is: /docs/CONTENT_VOICE_GUIDE.md

## STACK

- Next.js 14+ (App Router, NOT Pages Router)
- TypeScript (strict mode, no `any` types)
- Tailwind CSS (custom design tokens — see COLOR TOKENS below)
- Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage)
- Framer Motion (UI transitions, reveals)
- D3.js (Timeline, Celestial Map, interactive diagrams)
- Meilisearch (full-text search)
- Three.js — DEFERRED. Do not implement 3D. Build 2D versions first.

## COLOR TOKENS — USE THESE EXACTLY

```
--ink: #080604           /* deepest background, void */
--deep: #0d0a05          /* primary background */
--parchment-dark: #140f07 /* card backgrounds, elevated surfaces */
--gold: #c9a84c          /* primary accent — titles, icons, interactive */
--gold-light: #e8cc7d    /* hover states, emphasis */
--gold-pale: #f5e8c0     /* body text, readable content */
--gold-dim: #7a6230      /* disabled states, secondary borders */
--amber: #a06820         /* warm accent — warnings, highlights */
--ember: #7a3510         /* urgent, active states */
--blood: #5c1a10         /* danger, Abyss, ZAX references */
--ash: #3a3020           /* muted backgrounds, disabled text */
```

Do NOT use blue, green, purple, or any color outside this palette.
Do NOT use white (#ffffff) for backgrounds. The lightest background is --parchment-dark.
Body text color is --gold-pale. Never pure white text.

## TYPOGRAPHY

- Headings: Cinzel or Cormorant Garamond (serif)
- Body: Cormorant or EB Garamond (humanist serif)
- Monospace: JetBrains Mono or Fira Code (for glyphs and code)
- Do NOT use sans-serif fonts anywhere except very small UI labels

## WHAT CURSOR MUST OPTIMIZE FOR

1. Content readability — long-form text must be beautiful and comfortable
2. Badge visibility — epistemic badges must be legible inline, never hidden
3. Atmospheric feel — dark, warm, layered, deliberate
4. Mobile-first responsive — every component works on 375px width
5. Accessibility — WCAG 2.1 AA minimum, proper heading hierarchy, aria labels
6. Performance — no layout shift, lazy load images, code split by route

## WHAT CURSOR MUST NEVER DO

- Never invent content text. All prose comes from Claude's Markdown files.
- Never write placeholder lorem ipsum that could ship. Use "[CONTENT PENDING]" markers.
- Never use bright colors, gradients, or glassmorphism.
- Never add loading spinners. Use atmospheric fades (opacity transition, 300-600ms).
- Never use "Congratulations," "Level up," "Unlocked!" or any gamified language in UI.
- Never add features not in /docs/MVP_SCOPE.md without explicit human approval.
- Never implement Three.js, WebGL, or any 3D rendering. All immersive features are 2D.
- Never auto-generate glossary definitions, badge assignments, or historical claims.
- Never use localStorage or sessionStorage for critical state. User state goes to Supabase.
- Never override the design tokens. If a color doesn't exist in the palette, you don't need it.

## THE BADGE SYSTEM — IMPLEMENTATION RULES

The 8 epistemic badges: ◆ ◇ ○ △ ◎ ~ ? ⚠

- Badges are stored as metadata on content blocks, NOT as raw characters in rendered HTML.
- Render badges as styled <span> elements with consistent sizing (12-14px), the gold palette.
- Every badge has a tooltip showing its full name and meaning.
- Badges appear inline with claims in body text AND in sidebar/margin positions.
- Build a <Badge type="historical" /> component. Accepted types:
  historical, consensus, occult_claim, later_interpretation, parallel,
  speculative, disputed, caution
- The badge glossary must be accessible from any badge via click/tap.
- Future: badge filtering in search results. Design the data model to support this now.

## LESSON RENDERING RULES

Lessons are authored as Markdown in /content/curriculum/. The renderer must support:

- YAML frontmatter → metadata display (header table)
- ## headings → section navigation (left sidebar or top anchor bar)
- > blockquotes → styled as "Sidebar" cards (parchment-dark bg, gold-dim border)
- Tables → styled with the gold palette, no default browser table styling
- Badge glyphs inline → rendered via the <Badge /> component
- "Who Says This?" tables → distinct card styling, one card per claim
- Reflection Prompts → visually distinct section (slightly lighter background, italic prompt text)
- Knowledge Check → expandable/collapsible section, not quiz-engine — that's separate
- Closing Passage → full-width, centered, larger type, increased line-height

Lesson pages must include:
- Estimated reading time (calculated from word count)
- Section progress indicator (how far through the lesson)
- "Return to Initiation Path" persistent link
- Previous/Next lesson navigation at the bottom

## ROUTING STRUCTURE

```
/                           → Landing page
/archive                    → Archive pillar index
/archive/[entity-slug]      → Entity profile
/timeline                   → Interactive timeline
/map                        → Celestial Map (2D)
/language                   → Language Chamber
/path                       → Initiation Path dashboard
/path/[stage]/[lesson]      → Individual lesson (e.g., /path/seeker/1)
/aethyrs                    → Aethyr Journey index
/aethyrs/[number]           → Individual Aethyr
/observatory                → Intelligence Observatory
/glossary                   → Searchable glossary
/glossary/[term-slug]       → Individual term
/journal                    → Personal journal (auth required)
/search                     → Search results
/about                      → About the Archive
```

Use Next.js App Router conventions. Each route gets its own directory with page.tsx.
Dynamic routes use [slug] or [id] params.

## GLOSSARY BEHAVIOR

- Glossary terms appear as subtle gold underlines in lesson text
- Hover/tap shows a tooltip with the short definition
- Click navigates to the full glossary entry
- Glossary data lives in /lib/glossary.ts (will migrate to Supabase)
- New terms are ONLY added by Claude or the human. Never auto-generate definitions.

## SIDEBAR RULES

Sidebars in lessons (marked with > blockquotes and **Sidebar X — Title**) render as:

- Floating cards on desktop (right margin or inline-right, max-width 340px)
- Full-width collapsed cards on mobile (tap to expand)
- Background: --parchment-dark
- Border-left: 2px solid --gold-dim
- Title in --gold, body in --gold-pale
- Source citations in --gold-dim, smaller font

## MOBILE RULES

- All layouts must work at 375px minimum width
- Sidebars collapse to expandable cards
- Tables become horizontally scrollable or reflow to stacked cards
- The lens selector becomes a dropdown, not a tab bar
- Badge tooltips use bottom-sheet pattern on mobile, not hover
- Navigation collapses to a hamburger menu with the pillar list

## ANIMATION LIMITS

- Maximum transition duration: 600ms
- Preferred easing: ease-out or cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)
- Page transitions: fade-in only (opacity 0→1, 300ms)
- Content reveals: stagger children by 50ms, fade + slight translateY (8px)
- Scroll-triggered reveals: use IntersectionObserver, trigger at 0.1 threshold
- NO parallax scrolling
- NO infinite animations or looping effects
- NO particle effects (reserved for Phase 5 Three.js layer)
- NO animation on badges, glossary tooltips, or navigation elements

## WHAT REQUIRES HUMAN APPROVAL BEFORE BUILDING

(Gate 3 items from /docs/HUMAN_CONTROL_PROTOCOL.md)

- Any feature not listed in /docs/MVP_SCOPE.md
- Any change to the data model or database schema
- Any change to the badge system (types, display, behavior)
- Any change to the progression/rank system
- Any new route or pillar
- Any content about the Intelligence Observatory
- Any 3D or WebGL implementation
- Any change to the color palette or typography

## FILE CONVENTIONS

- Components: PascalCase (Badge.tsx, LessonRenderer.tsx)
- Utilities: camelCase (parseMarkdown.ts, getBadgeColor.ts)
- Routes: kebab-case directories (/path/seeker/page.tsx)
- Types: suffixed with Type or Props (LessonType, BadgeProps)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (BADGE_TYPES, RANK_ORDER)
- CSS: Tailwind utility classes only. No CSS modules. No styled-components.
- Tests: colocated with components (Badge.test.tsx next to Badge.tsx)

## COMMIT MESSAGE FORMAT

```
[pillar] short description

- detail 1
- detail 2
```

Example:
```
[lesson] implement lesson renderer with badge support

- parse YAML frontmatter from markdown
- render inline badges via Badge component
- add sidebar card styling for blockquotes
```

## QUICK REFERENCE — THE TEN COMMANDMENTS

1. Never present a claim without a badge
2. Never let a single lens dominate
3. Never sensationalize
4. Never moralize
5. Never use gamified language
6. Never break the 2D fallback requirement
7. Never ship content without a Source Pack
8. Never collapse the Archive into a single interpretation
9. Never sacrifice the reader's autonomy
10. Never build a feature not in MVP_SCOPE.md without Gate 3 approval
